clips/
.bundle/
# results.json is checked in: it is the evidence for the README. So are the
# bundle sizes, which have their own command because they need a build and no
# browser, and which the research page reads at build time like everything else.
results-*.json
!results-bundle.json
# And the command log, for the same reason: it is arithmetic over a data
# structure, so it needs no GPU and no clips, and re-taking it should not
# re-date the measurements that need both.
!results-log.json
# A tracked frame, which needs a tracking host most machines do not have, and
# how long a clip can be, which needs twenty minutes. Both have their own
# command for that reason and both are read by the research pages at build
# time, so a checkout without them cannot build the site.
!results-tracked-frame.json
!results-long-clip.json
# And where the sound goes in the file, which needs no GPU at all and answers a
# question about byte layout that shares nothing with the timings above.
!results-interleave.json
# And what the log costs once it has to become a file, which is the same class
# as results-log.json and is kept apart from it so that re-taking one does not
# re-date the other.
!results-document.json
# And what writing the log down on every edit costs, which is a question about
# the origin private file system and shares nothing with either of the two
# above.
!results-recovery.json
# And the export ladder, which is the only measurement in `all` that puts a
# STYLE CHAIN under the encoder: a change to a style makes it stale and makes
# nothing else in that run stale, so re-taking it together with the rest meant
# re-timing an ONNX session and a readback ladder that had not moved.
!results-export.json
